# PHASE 3: the touch overlay, driven by REAL TOUCH under phone emulation.
#
#   python3 -m http.server 8000 --directory web/dist-m1 &
#   node web/tools/drive-browser.mjs --headed --mobile 915,412,3 \
#        --out /tmp/touch-chrome \
#        --url http://localhost:8000/armagetronad.html \
#        --script-file web/tools/touch-gate.steps
#   kill %1
#
# 915x412 at dpr 3 is a Pixel-class Android phone held in LANDSCAPE (412x915 is
# its portrait CSS viewport), and 915*3 x 412*3 = 2745x1236 = 3.39 Mpx is the
# backing store the shell's sizing block should compute from it.
#
# WHAT IS REAL HERE AND WHAT IS NOT. The taps are real: Input.dispatchTouchEvent
# goes through the browser's own input pipeline and the page receives
# touchstart/pointerdown with isTrusted TRUE. What is synthetic is the thing
# under test -- the KeyboardEvent the overlay builds in response. That is the
# right way round; a test that synthesized the tap too would be watching the
# page talk to itself.
#
# EMULATION IS NOT A DEVICE. It supplies the viewport, the device pixel ratio
# and touch events, and nothing about phone GPU throughput, memory or thermals.
# The frame-rate dump at the end is THIS MACHINE's frame rate at a phone's pixel
# count, which is a different quantity from a phone's. Read the header of
# web/tools/drive-browser.mjs before quoting it.
#
# THE ROUTE IS THE FIRST-RUN FLOW, ON PURPOSE. A fresh profile lands on the
# language menu, then First Setup, then a welcome message, then the tutorial
# match -- and every one of those is dismissed here by TAPPING the on-screen
# pad. A visitor who cannot get past the language menu has not been given mobile
# support, so "the menus are reachable" is a gate condition and not a nicety.
#
# ROUND 2 MOVED THE CONTROLS AND THIS FILE MOVED WITH THEM. The Enter button is
# gone: Enter is now a tap anywhere on the screen, so every step that used to
# tap the pad's Enter button now taps the tap layer instead. Escape moved to a
# button in the top-left corner. The transcript therefore differs from Phase
# 3's line for line by design; what has NOT changed is what is asserted -- the
# menus are reachable by taps alone, both halves steer, and the game's own
# tooltip counters are the witness.
#
# T1b IS THE M6 SETTINGS CHANGE, AND IT IS ONLY HALF A CHECK ON ITS OWN. The
# page appends SPARKS 0 to the preloaded autoexec.cfg on a touch device, because
# the spark bursts thrown at a wall cost about a quarter of the frame
# (docs/evidence/m6-lag/task8-sparks/). This run asserts the append happened AND
# reads the file back; web/tools/menu-gate.steps asserts the desktop file is
# untouched. Read them as a pair -- the claim is "on touch and nowhere else".
#
# T1c IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME SETTING, ADDED AFTER THE MAINTAINER FOUND
# THAT ?sparks=1 DID NOT BRING THEM BACK. SPARKS persists to user.cfg, so the
# override has to WRITE rather than stay quiet; T1c runs last, on a session that
# has already saved SPARKS 0, and checks that a reload with ?sparks=1 puts
# SPARKS 1 in the file the game reads after user.cfg. Its own section says why.
#
# AND IT GAINED THE ONE CHECK THE NEW LAYOUT NEEDS: that a tap can never mean
# both things at once. T2b and T3b assert it STRUCTURALLY rather than by
# sampling behaviour -- in a menu the two turn zones have no box at all, and
# during a round the tap layer has none, because .aa-driving on the container
# display:none's whichever the game is not asking for. Two controls that are
# never in the box tree at the same time cannot collide.
#
# T1c RELOADS, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS LAST, for two reasons that are both about
# the navigation:
#
#   1. THE RELOAD DESTROYS BOTH PROBES. window.__fps and window.__tg are page
#      globals; a navigation takes them with it. Everything that reads them --
#      T1 to T6, and the frame-rate dump above all -- therefore has to have
#      already run. T1c's own checks are self-contained one-liners for the same
#      reason: there is nothing left to install into after the reload.
#   2. `until:` COUNTS THE WHOLE TRANSCRIPT, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT USEFUL
#      HERE. It does not reset at a navigation, so the counts run on: this
#      script boots the game TWICE (the first load and T1c's reload with
#      ?sparks=1), and each `until:` names its own ordinal.
#
# The driver survives the navigation -- it attaches to a TARGET, not to a
# document, so the console stream and Runtime.evaluate carry on into the new
# page. That has held since Phase 3, when web/tools/touch-portrait-probe.steps
# reloaded mid-script too; M7 deleted that probe along with the portrait-hold
# flow it drove, so T1c below is this file's only navigation and M7's portrait
# gate (web/tools/portrait-boot-gate.steps) has none at all -- it rotates the
# emulated phone and never reloads. ONE driver run covers the whole cycle. What
# must not happen is an `eval:` in the window where the old execution context is
# gone and the new one has not arrived, so every step after a navigation waits on
# `until:` first -- which reads the transcript and touches no execution context
# at all.
#
# --headed is REQUIRED, for the reason in drive-browser.mjs's header.

wait:1000

# The frame sampler, copied verbatim from web/tools/fps-resolution-probe.steps
# so the numbers are the same quantity M2 and M5 measured. It counts the game's
# own glFlush/glFinish calls, i.e. buffer swaps.
eval:(()=>{if(window.__fps)return 'already installed';const S={t:new Float64Array(400000),n:0,marks:[],gl:[],polls:0};window.__fps=S;const wrap=(C)=>{if(!C)return;for(const m of ['flush','finish']){const o=C.prototype[m];if(!o)continue;C.prototype[m]=function(){if(S.n<S.t.length)S.t[S.n++]=performance.now();if(S.n%30===0){S.polls++;const e=this.getError();if(e){S.gl.push([S.n,e]);if(S.gl.length<=20)console.error('[GLERR] frame '+S.n+' glGetError=0x'+e.toString(16));}}return o.apply(this,arguments);};}};wrap(window.WebGLRenderingContext);wrap(window.WebGL2RenderingContext);const cl=console.log.bind(console);console.log=function(){const s=(arguments.length&&typeof arguments[0]==='string')?arguments[0]:'';if(s.indexOf('[L] ')===0)S.marks.push([performance.now(),s]);return cl.apply(null,arguments);};return 'frame sampler armed: glFlush+glFinish counted, glGetError polled every 30th frame, [L] lines timestamped'})()

# The tooltip-counter probe, as in web/tools/synthetic-key-gate.steps.
eval:(()=>{const S={};S.rd=(p)=>{try{return Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){return null}};S.line=(t,k)=>{if(t===null)return null;const m=t.split('\n').find(l=>{const s=l.trim();return s.indexOf(k)===0&&/\s/.test(s.charAt(k.length))});return m?m.trim().slice(k.length).trim().replace(/\s+/g,' '):null};S.save=()=>{try{Module._aa_web_save_config();return 'saved'}catch(e){return 'SAVE FAILED: '+e}};S.box=(id)=>{const e=document.getElementById(id);if(!e)return 'missing';const r=e.getBoundingClientRect();return Math.round(r.width)+'x'+Math.round(r.height)+'@'+Math.round(r.left)+','+Math.round(r.top)};S.dump=(phase)=>{const sv=S.save();const c=S.rd('/persist/var/user.cfg');const cv=document.getElementById('canvas');const ctx=window.AA_INPUT_CONTEXT?window.AA_INPUT_CONTEXT():null;console.log('[TOUCHGATE] '+JSON.stringify({phase:phase,save:sv,ctx:ctx,ctx_menu:ctx===null?null:!!(ctx&1),ctx_cycle:ctx===null?null:!!(ctx&2),driving_class:document.getElementById('touch').classList.contains('aa-driving'),box_tapzone:S.box('tapzone'),box_tzleft:S.box('tz-left'),box_tzright:S.box('tz-right'),box_escbtn:S.box('escbtn'),box_touchpad:S.box('touchpad'),canvas_w:cv.width,canvas_h:cv.height,mpx:Math.round(cv.width*cv.height/1e4)/100,css_w:Math.round(cv.getBoundingClientRect().width),css_h:Math.round(cv.getBoundingClientRect().height),inner:window.innerWidth+'x'+window.innerHeight,dpr:window.devicePixelRatio,touch_hidden:document.getElementById('touch').hidden,chip_hidden:document.getElementById('reloadchip').hidden,maxTouchPoints:navigator.maxTouchPoints,coarse:window.matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)').matches,tip_left:S.line(c,'CYCLE_TURN_LEFT_TOOLTIP'),tip_right:S.line(c,'CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT_TOOLTIP')}));return phase};window.__tg=S;return 'probe installed'})()

until:1:90000:[BOOT] autostart
mark:GAME-STARTED

# ===========================================================================
# L1: LANDSCAPE IS THE LAYOUT IT ALWAYS WAS. M7 added a second layout (a square
# picture with a pad below it) for a portrait LOAD. This is the negative half:
# on the same device held the other way up, none of it turns on.
#
# THREE CLAIMS AND A REFERENCE. The claims are the three switches M7 added,
# each of which can fail on its own: the class the whole layout hangs off
# (aa-gameboy, absent), the pad (display:none, so its six buttons are not in
# the tree the player can hit), and --aa-square, which sizeCanvas writes in
# BOTH branches -- 0px here, its true answer when there is no square. That last
# one is why a reader can take the variable at face value instead of having to
# tell "no square" apart from "never set", and no run before this one had
# proven the 0px half.
#
# `visible` is a reference rather than a claim: the id (or class) of every
# descendant of #touch whose OWN computed display is not none, in document
# order, snapshotted at the language menu. Committed as
# docs/evidence/m7-gameboy/landscape-visible-reference.txt; a future run that
# adds, loses or re-hides a control here changes that string, and a human diff
# is a better tool for "did the landscape overlay move" than a check that would
# fail on every legitimate addition.
#
# READ IT AS A TREE, NOT AS A PICTURE. getComputedStyle answers for the element
# it is given, so an element hidden by an ANCESTOR still lists here: the pad's
# six buttons are in the measured string even though #pad itself is display:none
# and nothing of it is painted. That is why pad_display is a separate field and
# is the one in the PASS. What the string does show directly is the elements the
# cascade hides itself -- tz-left and tz-right are absent, because .aa-driving is
# off in a menu -- and that is also why it is not in the PASS: it is only stable
# at a fixed point in the script.
# ===========================================================================
eval:(()=>{const t=document.getElementById('touch');const vis=[...t.querySelectorAll('*')].filter(e=>getComputedStyle(e).display!=='none').map(e=>e.id||e.className).join(',');const r={layout:document.documentElement.classList.contains('aa-gameboy')?'gameboy':'full',visible:vis,pad_display:getComputedStyle(document.getElementById('pad')).display,square_var:getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--aa-square').trim()};r.PASS=r.layout==='full'&&r.pad_display==='none'&&r.square_var==='0px';console.log('[M7GATE] L1 landscape-unchanged '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'L1 '+r.PASS})()

wait:9000

# T1: the page decided this is a touch device, sized the canvas from the phone
# viewport, and is showing the controls.
eval:window.__tg.dump('landscape-at-language-menu')
shot:00-language-menu-with-touch-controls

# ===========================================================================
# T1b: THE CRASH SPARKS ARE OFF, AND THE FILE THE GAME PARSES SAYS SO.
#
# WHY THE SETTING IS THERE. M6 task 8 measured the spark bursts thrown while a
# cycle grinds a wall -- which on a phone is not a contrived case, it is what a
# thumb does by accident every round. Stock: 17.15 ms median frame, 20.3 ms in
# the worst measured second, the draw count bursting to 171 calls in a frame.
# The same arm with SPARKS 0, twice: 13.1 and 13.65 ms median, worst 13.8 and
# 15.0, draws pinned flat at 60. About a quarter of the frame at the wall --
# read with its condition: that 17.15 is the arm whose cycle sparked in 29 of
# its 40 rim seconds, and the second stock run of the same arm sparked in only
# 11 of 40 and read 14.0 ms median (worst 18.3).
# docs/evidence/m6-lag/task8-sparks/.
#
# TWO CHECKS, BECAUSE EITHER ALONE CAN PASS WHILE THE GAME IS UNCHANGED. The
# `until:` is the page saying it performed the append; the `eval:` READS THE
# FILE BACK through Module.FS, so what is asserted is what the game's config
# parser will see and not what the page logged about itself. The count of
# SPARKS lines is asserted to be exactly one: a second one would mean the
# append ran twice, which the last-one-wins parser would hide.
#
# THE OTHER HALF OF THIS CHECK IS ON THE DESKTOP GATE. It is touch-only by
# design, so web/tools/menu-gate.steps reads the same file after a desktop boot
# and asserts the word SPARKS does not appear in it at all. Neither run proves
# the change on its own; the pair does.
# ===========================================================================
until:1:30000:[SPARKS] SPARKS 0 written to /data/webdefaults/autoexec.cfg
eval:(()=>{const p='/data/webdefaults/autoexec.cfg';let t=null,err=null;try{t=Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){err=String(e)}const ok=typeof t==='string';const n=ok?(t.match(/^SPARKS\b/gm)||[]).length:-1;const ends=ok&&/\nSPARKS 0\n$/.test(t);return '[SPARKSGATE] T1b sparks-off-on-touch '+JSON.stringify({read:ok,err:err,bytes:ok?t.length:0,sparks_lines:n,ends_with_sparks_0:ends,tail:ok?t.slice(-96):null,PASS:ok&&n===1&&ends})})()

# ===========================================================================
# T2: THE MENUS, BY TAPPING ONLY. No key: step appears anywhere below.
# ===========================================================================
# T2b: THE COLLISION CHECK, MENU SIDE. Before the first tap, while a menu is on
# screen: the game reports no cycle, the container does not carry .aa-driving,
# and BOTH turn zones have a zero box -- so the tap below cannot also be a turn,
# and (new in round 2) a tap in a settings menu can no longer send
# SDLK_LEFT/SDLK_RIGHT into items[selected]->LeftRight().
eval:(()=>{const t=document.getElementById('touch');const z=['tz-left','tz-right'].map(i=>document.getElementById(i).getBoundingClientRect());const p=document.getElementById('tapzone').getBoundingClientRect();const ctx=window.AA_INPUT_CONTEXT();return '[TOUCHGATE] T2b menu-side collision check '+JSON.stringify({ctx:ctx,driving_class:t.classList.contains('aa-driving'),turn_zones_have_no_box:z.every(r=>r.width===0&&r.height===0),tap_layer_area:Math.round(p.width*p.height),PASS:!t.classList.contains('aa-driving')&&z.every(r=>r.width===0&&r.height===0)&&p.width>0})})()

mark:TAP-ENTER-ACCEPTS-LANGUAGE
tap:#tapzone
until:1:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:2500
shot:01-first-setup-reached-by-tapping

# "Accept" is the top item of First Setup and is already selected on arrival
# (see docs/evidence/phase3-touch/synkey-... screenshots), so one Enter leaves.
mark:TAP-ENTER-ACCEPTS-FIRST-SETUP
tap:#tapzone
until:2:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:3000
shot:02-welcome-message-reached-by-tapping

# uMenu::Message has its own event loop and is dismissed by a keypress.
mark:TAP-ENTER-DISMISSES-WELCOME
tap:#tapzone
wait:2000

until:1:90000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-1-STARTED-WITHOUT-A-SINGLE-KEYSTROKE
wait:2000
shot:03-round1-driving
eval:window.__tg.dump('round1-before-steering')

# ===========================================================================
# T3: STEERING, BY TAPPING THE TWO HALVES OF THE SCREEN.
# The witness is the game's own uActionTooltip counters, which start at
# "0 2 1 1 1" and "0 3 1 1 1" and are decremented only by a turn the player's
# cycle object accepted and executed.
# ===========================================================================
# T3b: THE COLLISION CHECK, ROUND SIDE. The mirror of T2b: the game reports a
# live cycle and no menu, the container carries .aa-driving, and the tap layer
# has a zero box -- so a tap on the screen during a round steers and cannot
# confirm anything.
eval:(()=>{const t=document.getElementById('touch');const p=document.getElementById('tapzone').getBoundingClientRect();const z=['tz-left','tz-right'].map(i=>document.getElementById(i).getBoundingClientRect());const ctx=window.AA_INPUT_CONTEXT();return '[TOUCHGATE] T3b round-side collision check '+JSON.stringify({ctx:ctx,driving_class:t.classList.contains('aa-driving'),tap_layer_has_no_box:p.width===0&&p.height===0,turn_zone_areas:z.map(r=>Math.round(r.width*r.height)),PASS:t.classList.contains('aa-driving')&&p.width===0&&p.height===0&&z.every(r=>r.width>0)})})()

mark:TAP-LEFT-HALF
tap:#tz-left:3
wait:800
shot:04-after-tapping-left-half
eval:window.__tg.dump('after-tapping-left-half')

mark:TAP-RIGHT-HALF
tap:#tz-right:4
wait:800
shot:05-after-tapping-right-half
eval:window.__tg.dump('after-tapping-right-half')

# A second round, for the same reason the synthetic-key gate has one: a press
# only counts while the player has a live cycle, and the human dies to the AI
# in a few seconds.
until:2:150000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-2-STARTED
wait:2000
tap:#tz-left:3
wait:700
tap:#tz-right:4
wait:800
shot:06-after-round2-taps
eval:window.__tg.dump('after-round2-taps')

# A THIRD ROUND, RIGHT ONLY, BECAUSE THE RIGHT COUNTER STARTS AT 3 AND THE
# HUMAN DIES IN A FEW SECONDS. Phase 3 spent all four counters; the round-2
# sequence above regularly spends only one or two of the right-hand three
# because the cycle is already dead by the third tap -- which is a property of
# how long the AI takes to win, not of the overlay. Round 3 exists so
# CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT_TOOLTIP reaches 0 the way it did in Phase 3's transcript.
until:3:150000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-3-STARTED-RIGHT-TAPS-ONLY
wait:2000
tap:#tz-right:2
wait:700
tap:#tz-right:2
wait:800
shot:06b-after-round3-right-taps
eval:window.__tg.dump('after-round3-right-taps')

# ===========================================================================
# T4: ORIENTATION. Rotating the emulated phone to portrait MID-GAME shows the
# reload chip and nothing else: no prompt, no hold, the game keeps running.
# ===========================================================================
until:1:150000:[L] ROUND_WINNER
mark:ROTATE-TO-PORTRAIT
metrics:412:915:3
wait:1500
eval:(()=>{const chip=document.getElementById('reloadchip');const rot=document.getElementById('rotate');const r={chip_visible:!!chip&&!chip.hidden,rotate_element:rot!==null,canvas_w:document.getElementById('canvas').width,canvas_h:document.getElementById('canvas').height};r.PASS=r.chip_visible&&!r.rotate_element;console.log('[M7GATE] T4 rotate-to-portrait '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'T4 '+r.PASS})()
shot:07-portrait-mid-game-shows-only-the-chip
mark:ROTATE-BACK-TO-LANDSCAPE
metrics:915:412:3
wait:1500
eval:(()=>{const chip=document.getElementById('reloadchip');const r={chip_hidden:!!chip&&chip.hidden};r.PASS=r.chip_hidden;console.log('[M7GATE] T4 back-in-landscape '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'T4b '+r.PASS})()
shot:08-back-in-landscape

# ===========================================================================
# T5: the frame rate, dumped exactly as fps-resolution-probe.steps dumps it.
# ===========================================================================
mark:FRAME-RATE
eval:(()=>{const S=window.__fps;if(!S)return 'no sampler';const all=Array.prototype.slice.call(S.t.subarray(0,S.n));const nr=S.marks.filter(m=>m[1].indexOf('[L] NEW_ROUND')===0).map(m=>m[0]);const rw=S.marks.filter(m=>m[1].indexOf('[L] ROUND_WINNER')===0).map(m=>m[0]);const q=(a,p)=>{if(!a.length)return null;const s=a.slice().sort((x,y)=>x-y);return s[Math.min(s.length-1,Math.max(0,Math.round(p*(s.length-1))))]};const r2=(x)=>(x===null||x===undefined)?null:Math.round(x*100)/100;const f=(v)=>(v&&v>0)?Math.round(100000/v)/100:null;const stats=(t0,t1)=>{const w=all.filter(x=>x>=t0&&x<=t1);const d=[];for(let i=1;i<w.length;i++)d.push(w[i]-w[i-1]);const sec={};for(const x of w){const k=Math.floor((x-t0)/1000);sec[k]=(sec[k]||0)+1}const span=(t1-t0)/1000;const nb=Math.floor(span);const per=[];for(let k=0;k<nb;k++)per.push(sec[k]||0);return{span_s:r2(span),frames:w.length,mean_fps:span>0?r2(w.length/span):null,frame_ms:{p50:r2(q(d,0.5)),p90:r2(q(d,0.9)),p99:r2(q(d,0.99)),max:r2(q(d,1))},inst_fps:{median:f(q(d,0.5)),p10:f(q(d,0.9)),p01:f(q(d,0.99)),min:f(q(d,1))},fps_per_second:per,fps_per_second_min:per.length?Math.min.apply(null,per):null,fps_per_second_median:q(per,0.5)}};const out={total_frames_sampled:S.n,rounds_started:nr.length,rounds_won:rw.length,gl_errors:S.gl.length,gl_polls:S.polls};const n=Math.min(nr.length,rw.length);if(n>0){out.overall=stats(nr[0],rw[n-1]);out.per_round=[];for(let i=0;i<n;i++)out.per_round.push(stats(nr[i],rw[i]))}return JSON.stringify(out)})()

# ===========================================================================
# T6: ESCAPE IN THE TOP-LEFT CORNER. Last, so whatever menu it opens cannot
# disturb the round-timing checks above. What is asserted is that the corner
# button is where it is supposed to be, is big enough to hit, and dispatches
# Escape -- the [TOUCH] lines and the menu that appears are the witness.
# Whether the corner fights Android's back gesture is NOT testable here and is
# not claimed; see the comment on the button's CSS in web/shell.html.
# ===========================================================================
mark:TAP-ESCAPE-IN-THE-CORNER
eval:(()=>{const r=document.getElementById('escbtn').getBoundingClientRect();return '[TOUCHGATE] T6 escape corner '+JSON.stringify({rect:Math.round(r.width)+'x'+Math.round(r.height)+'@'+Math.round(r.left)+','+Math.round(r.top),at_least_44px:r.width>=44&&r.height>=44,inset_from_left_px:Math.round(r.left),inset_from_top_px:Math.round(r.top),centre_x_px:Math.round(r.left+r.width/2)})})()
tap:#escbtn
wait:2500
shot:10-after-tapping-escape-in-the-corner
eval:window.__tg.dump('after-escape-corner-tap')

# ===========================================================================
# T1c: ?sparks=1 HAS TO BEAT THE VALUE THE GAME HAS ALREADY SAVED.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS IS THE REGRESSION TEST FOR, from the maintainer's phone:
# "?sparks=1 does not bring the sparks back". The first version of this change
# read ?sparks=1 as "skip the touch default", i.e. write NOTHING -- which is
# how ?cam=1 works and is wrong here, because SPARKS is not the camera.
#
# SPARKS is a tConfItem and tConfItemBase::Save() returns true
# (src/tools/tConfiguration.h:296), so st_SaveConfig writes it into
# /persist/var/user.cfg -- SaveAll right-aligns the title in a 28-column field
# (tConfiguration.cpp:473), so the line reads "                      SPARKS 0",
# which is why the check below matches with leading whitespace allowed. This
# page saves on every menu leave, so ONE session with the touch append in
# effect is enough to put SPARKS 0 in the player's own config. After that,
# writing nothing is not an override: st_LoadConfig reads user.cfg FIRST
# (tConfiguration.cpp:975) and the userconfigdir autoexec.cfg near the END
# (line 992), so silence leaves the saved 0 standing and the sparks stay off.
# The CAMERA_* items are tSettingItems, Save() returns false (line 497), they
# never reach user.cfg, and that is the whole reason ?cam=1's silence works.
#
# SO THE ORDER OF THIS SECTION IS THE TEST. It runs LAST, after a run that has
# already left several menus with the touch append in effect, and it checks the
# saved config FIRST: the precondition is that user.cfg really does hold
# SPARKS 0. Only then does it reload with ?sparks=1, and then it asks the GAME
# rather than the page -- it forces st_SaveConfig and reads the live value back
# out of user.cfg, where it comes back 1. A gate that only checked the second
# half would pass against a machine that had never saved anything, which is
# exactly the state in which this bug is invisible.
#
# location.search is assigned rather than a URL typed out, so this step does not
# have to know which port the server was started on.
# ===========================================================================
mark:T1c-PRECONDITION-THE-SAVED-CONFIG-HOLDS-SPARKS-0
eval:(()=>{const p='/persist/var/user.cfg';let t=null,err=null;try{t=Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){err=String(e)}const ok=typeof t==='string';const m=ok?/^[ \t]*SPARKS[ \t]+(\S+)[ \t]*$/m.exec(t):null;return '[SPARKSGATE] T1c precondition saved-config-holds-sparks-0 '+JSON.stringify({read:ok,err:err,bytes:ok?t.length:0,line:m?m[0]:null,value:m?m[1]:null,PASS:!!m&&m[1]==='0'})})()

mark:T1c-RELOAD-WITH-SPARKS-1
eval:location.search='?sparks=1'
until:1:90000:[SPARKS] SPARKS 1 written to /data/webdefaults/autoexec.cfg
until:2:90000:[BOOT] autostart
wait:8000
# THE CHECK ASKS THE GAME, NOT THE PAGE. Module._aa_web_save_config() is
# st_SaveConfig, which writes every tConfItem's LIVE value into user.cfg -- so
# forcing it here and reading SPARKS back out of that file is a reading of what
# the running game believes, not of what this page wrote a moment earlier. If
